    },
    sections: [
      {
        type: 'about',
        tabLabel: 'About',
        pages: [
          {
            title: 'Eight Off Solitaire',
            subtitle: 'Patience',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "Eight Off is a sophisticated and highly winnable solitaire card game that served as a direct precursor to the ubiquitous FreeCell. Its defining feature is the namesake eight 'free cells' (reserves)â€”twice the number found in its more famous descendant. However, this extra mobility is balanced by a stricter building rule: cards in the tableau must be built down by the CORRECT SUIT, rather than alternating colors. This makes Eight Off a pure game of logic and strategy where almost every deal can be solved with perfect planning.",
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "Eight Off rose to prominence in the mid-20th century as part of the family of solitaire games that utilize 'cells' for temporary storage. It is often credited with inspiring Paul Alfille's creation of FreeCell in 1978.",
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'One player only. Eight Off is a patience (solitaire) game played by a single person; there are no opponents or partners.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Single standard 52-card deck; all cards dealt face up into eight tableau columns and four reserve cells at the start.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Standard deck and a playing surface; no other equipment required.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Deal face up: six cards to each of eight tableau columns, and the remaining four cards into the first four reserve (free) cells.',
              },
            ],
          },
        ],
      },
    ],
